        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "6a5750fc-8e57-5c76-90d1-a8653c1082bb",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Croydon Airport, Gatwick Airport, Earlswood, South London, south-east England, Central London, Crawley, Redhill, Horley, Whitebushes, Brussels-Evere Airport, Belgium\n- Products: 1921 SNETA Farman Goliath\n- Concepts: ICAO codes, IATA codes, airport replacement, airport traffic ranking\n\nFacts:\n- Croydon Airport, with ICAO code EGCR, was an airport in South London, England.\n- Croydon Airport was London’s main airport before Northolt Aerodrome, London Heathrow Airport, and Gatwick Airport replaced it.\n- A 1921 SNETA Farman Goliath mail flight operated from Croydon Airport in the United Kingdom to Brussels-Evere Airport in Belgium.\n- Gatwick Airport, also called London Gatwick, has IATA code LGW and ICAO code EGKK.\n- Gatwick Airport is a major international airport in south-east England, 29.5 miles south of Central London and 2.7 nautical miles north of Crawley.\n- Gatwick Airport is the eighth-busiest airport in Europe.\n- Earlswood lies on the A23 between Redhill toward London and Horley, which is next to Gatwick Airport.\n- Whitebushes is a neighbouring community separated from Earlswood by a wide rural buffer zone.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "10d2b72c-98c7-5150-9bd0-5634f3c43de5",
        "text": "Page: Croydon Airport. Sentence: Croydon Airport (ICAO: EGCR ) was an airport in South London, England.\nPage: Croydon Airport. Sentence: It was the main airport for London before it was replaced by Northolt Aerodrome, London Heathrow Airport and Gatwick Airport.\nPage: Gatwick Airport. Sentence: Gatwick Airport (also known as London Gatwick) (IATA: LGW, ICAO: EGKK) is a major international airport in south-east England, 29.5 mi south of Central London and 2.7 NM north of Crawley.\nPage: Gatwick Airport. Sentence: Gatwick is the eighth-busiest airport in Europe.\nPage: 1921 SNETA Farman Goliath ditching. Sentence: The aircraft was operating a mail flight from Croydon Airport, United Kingdom to Brussels-Evere Airport, Belgium.\nPage: Earlswood. Sentence: Earlswood lies on the A23 between Redhill (in the direction of London) and Horley (next to Gatwick Airport), from which the neighbouring community, Whitebushes is separated by a wide rural buffer zone.",
